  "content": {
    "title": "The Term '67' Is 'Impossible to Define.' It Just Became Dictionary.com's Word of the Year for 2025",
    "text": "The dictionary says the viral word, which has become particularly popular among members of Gen Alpha, is “meaningless, ubiquitous and nonsensical” Since June, searches for \"67\" on Dictionary.com have surged more than sixfold. iStock via Getty Images It’s rare for a dictionary to claim that a word has no definition. But that’s what Dictionary.com said about its recently announced word of the year: “67,” pronounced “six-seven,” the slang term that took over the algorithms, classrooms and homes of kids and teenagers this summer—much to the dismay of the confused adults around them. “Perhaps the most defining feature of ‘67’ is that it’s impossible to define,” says the dictionary in a statement. “It’s meaningless, ubiquitous and nonsensical.​​ In other words, it has all the hallmarks of brain rot.” (“Brain rot,” by the way, was Oxford University Press’ word of the year in 2024.) The term “67,” which is often accompanied with a flat-palmed, see-sawing hand gesture that looks somewhat like how a cartoon character might shrug, or perhaps a bad attempt at juggling, has become a calling card for the young and online. It’s also become a headache for adults. One teacher on TikTok warned fellow instructors to avoid counting out loud in class, unless they wanted to be bombarded by an entire classroom shouting “67,” reports Elise Solé for TODAY.com. Another teacher tells TODAY.com that she banned the word entirely. If students break her rule, they have to write a 67-word essay about the term’s meaning. That would be difficult, because the point of the term is that it has no meaning. It’s sometimes deployed for the purpose of eliciting confusion, and the response itself becomes the punchline. Merriam-Webster defines it as “a nonsensical expression.” NPR’s Rachel Treisman calls it “basically one big inside joke.” Report AdThe term can be traced back to the song “Doot Doot (6 7)” by the rapper Skrilla, in which he refers to 67th Street in Philadelphia, per the Wall Street Journal’s Ellen Gamerman. The song was then paired with video edits of LaMelo Ball, a star NBA point guard on the Charlotte Hornets who stands—you guessed it—six feet and seven inches tall. A viral video of a boy at a basketball game doing the “67” hand motion pushed the phenomenon further into the spotlight. Even the rapper who coined the term can’t provide a definition. “I never put an actual meaning on it, and I still would not want to,” he tells the Wall Street Journal. “That’s why everybody keeps saying it.” Searches for the term “experienced a dramatic rise beginning in the summer of 2025,” according to Dictionary.com. “Since June, those searches have increased more than sixfold, and so far the surge shows no signs of stopping.” The word has had impressive staying power for nearly the entire year, middle school teacher and comedian Philip Lindsay, who first posted on Instagram about the trend in February, tells NPR. That’s uncommon for a viral meme. But he wonders if the Dictionary.com accolade might change that. Report Ad“I would imagine after this designation and more people using it, that it is going to start to become cringe to the younger generation,” he tells NPR. “However, I do think that because they are numbers that are seen everywhere, it is going to live on for quite some time in classrooms and in the minds of Gen Alpha.” Words on Dictionary.com’s shortlist included “agentic,” “kiss cam,” “tariff” and “tradwife,” among others. The dictionary’s words of the year in 2024 and 2023 were “demure” and “hallucinate,” respectively. Quick fact: What is the official definition of “demure”? Dictionary.com defines the word as “characterized by shyness and modesty; reserved.” Not every adult has frowned upon the term’s proliferation. Some families dressed as “67” for Halloween, and some teachers have embraced the word in the classroom. In September, kindergarten teacher Sara James posted a TikTok of her counting to ten and doing the hand gesture when she got to seven; her classroom erupts in excitement, USA Today’s Greta Cross reports. “Whatever they like, I’m down for,” the teacher wrote in her caption. “As long as it keeps them engaged.” You Might Also Like Get the latest stories in your inbox every weekday. More about: children Internet Language linguistics Technology teens Trending Today",
